                              THE BLADE



    “I’m here,” she said. Quietly, evenly. “I’m not going anywhere. You
tell me where you are and I’ll stay.” A pause. “No, honey. I don’t need
to know why. I’m listening.” Another pause. “That’s all right. Take your
time. I’m not going anywhere.”
    She stayed on the line for what must have been twenty minutes, her
eyes on the wall, her hand flat on the table, holding it. The women on
either side kept connecting calls without once looking at her. It was un‐
derstood. Somebody had a person on the line.
    Then the exchange thinned out, and the folding table came back,
and a kid in a lanyard looked at me from the floor.
    I’d told myself a man in headphones was a spy. Maybe he was. I had
just broken a boy’s ear for doing the one thing I do all day.



Danny didn’t go to the ER.
   He took the Gatorade, and the ice, and the bandage Manny made
from a clean T-shirt, and he told us, in a voice that came and went, why.
“If I go, they see the bill. They see the bill, they see the window. They
ask what happened. I tell them an equipment surge. They know it
wasn’t an equipment surge.” He swallowed. “I need this job. I’m in
school. I do my homework in this room.”
   “I’ll pay,” I said.
   “With what?” He wasn’t being cruel. “You sold your synth. I heard
the whole thing through the pipe. I’m sorry. It was a good synth.”
   I shut my mouth.
   He gave in at four in the morning, when Yari called back from the
break room and talked him into a walk-in clinic that opened at six, a
place she knew, a doctor who didn’t log insurance in the system Aper‐
ture could see. He said okay. I took down the address.
   And while we waited, because a person on a floor at 4 a.m. will tell
the truth if nobody makes him, he told us what he did.
